---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ToolRoster from '../../components/ToolRoster.astro';
import RelatedTools from '@kit/components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { SITE, toolBySlug, toolsExcept } from '../../lib/site';

const tool = toolBySlug('final-grade-calculator')!;
const canonical = `/tools/${tool.slug}`;
const title = `${tool.title} | ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'Work out the mark you need on the final exam. Enter what each part of the ' +
  'course is worth and what you have scored, and it tells you what the final ' +
  'has to carry — including when no mark is enough.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: tool.title, url: `${SITE.url}${canonical}`,
    applicationCategory: 'EducationalApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question', name: 'How do I calculate what I need on my final?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text:
          'Subtract the marks you have already banked, as a share of the whole course, from your ' +
          'target. Divide the remainder by the weight of the final. If homework is 20% and you ' +
          'scored 92, you have banked 18.4 points of the course; the final has to supply the rest.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question', name: 'What if I need more than 100% on the final?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text:
          'Then the target is out of reach and no mark on the exam will get you there. This is a ' +
          'common outcome at the point in term when people search for this, and it is worth ' +
          'knowing early — either revise the target, or ask about extra credit before the exam ' +
          'rather than after it.' },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema}>
  <ToolRoster
    title="What do I need on the final?"
    intro="Enter every graded part of the course and what it is worth. Leave the final's score blank — that is what we are solving for."
    breadcrumbs={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/tools', label: 'Calculators' }, { label: 'Final grade' }]}
    rosterId="fin"
    addLabel="Add a component"
    columns="minmax(0,1fr) 96px 96px"
  >
    <Fragment slot="result">
      <div class="scorecard__label" id="fin-label">Needed on the final</div>
      <div class="scorecard__value" id="fin-need">—</div>
      <p class="scorecard__sub" id="fin-sub">—</p>
      <div class="scorecard__split">
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="stat__label">Standing now</span>
          <span class="scorecard__alt" id="fin-cur">—</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <span class="stat__label">Best possible</span>
          <span class="scorecard__alt" id="fin-best">—</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="options">
      <div class="field" style="margin-top:var(--s-4)">
        <label class="field__label" for="fin-target">
          <span>Target grade</span>
          <span class="field__hint">the final course mark you want</span>
        </label>
        <div class="field__control" data-affix="post">
          <input class="input" id="fin-target" type="text" inputmode="decimal" value="90" />
          <span class="field__affix field__affix--post" aria-hidden="true">%</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="roster">
      <span>Component</span><span>Weight %</span><span>Your score %</span>
    </Fragment>

    <div class="working">
      <div class="working__head">Where the marks are</div>
      <div class="working__body" id="fin-working"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="verdict">
      <div class="verdict__label">In one sentence</div>
      <p class="verdict__text" id="fin-verdict">—</p>
    </div>

    <div id="fin-notes"></div>

    <section class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s-8)">
      <h2>The arithmetic, in one paragraph</h2>
      <p>
        Every graded component is worth a share of the course. Multiply what
        you scored by what it was worth, and you get the points you have banked
        toward the whole course — not toward that assignment. Homework worth
        20% with a score of 92 banks 18.4 points out of 100. Do that for every
        graded item, add them up, and subtract the total from your target. What
        is left is what the final has to supply, and dividing it by the final's
        weight gives the mark you need on the exam itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The distinction that trips people up: your <em>current grade</em> is
        your marks divided by the weight <em>graded so far</em>. Your
        <em>banked points</em> are your marks as a share of the
        <em>whole course</em>. If you are sitting at 84% with half the course
        graded, you have banked 42 points, not 84. Both numbers are shown above
        because confusing them is how people badly misjudge what the exam has
        to do.
      </p>

      <h2>The two answers people are not expecting</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>You need more than 100%.</strong> The target is out of reach —
        no mark on the exam will get you there. Most calculators print "you
        need 118%" and leave you to work out what that means. That is
        technically true and practically useless, so this one says the target
        is unreachable and shows the best grade still available to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is worth knowing before the exam rather than after. If a
        particular grade matters — a scholarship condition, a prerequisite, a
        GPA threshold — the time to ask about extra credit, a reweighting, or a
        late submission is while the course is still running.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>You need zero or less.</strong> The grade is already secured:
        even a blank exam leaves you above the target. Students revising in a
        panic for an exam they mathematically cannot fail deserve to know that,
        and the tool says so plainly rather than printing a negative percentage
        and letting you puzzle over it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this cannot know</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Dropped scores.</strong> Many courses drop the lowest quiz or
        homework mark. If yours does, leave the dropped item out entirely
        rather than entering a zero — including it will understate where you
        stand.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Curves and scaling.</strong> A curve applied to the final, or
        to the course as a whole, changes the answer and is not knowable in
        advance. Treat the figure here as what you need before any curve, which
        is the conservative direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Rounding rules.</strong> Whether an 89.5 becomes an A- depends
        on the instructor, and some do not round at all. If you land within
        half a point of a boundary, ask rather than assume.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Weights that do not add to 100.</strong> If your components sum
        to less than 100%, the shortfall is treated as the final's weight. If
        they sum to more, something has been entered twice. The page flags both
        rather than quietly producing a number.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the final is worth less than it feels</h2>
      <p>
        A final exam looms larger in the mind than it does in the arithmetic.
        On a course where the exam is worth 30%, the difference between a 70
        and a 90 on it is six points of your course grade. That is real, and it
        is usually not the difference between letter grades — which means the
        marks you have already banked are doing most of the work, and always
        were.
      </p>
      <p>
        The corollary is more useful: a heavily weighted final is the one worth
        worrying about. If the exam is 50% or more of the course, it genuinely
        can move you two letter grades, and the calculation above is telling
        you something that matters. Check the weight before you decide how
        much to panic.
      </p>

      <h2>Weighted categories versus straight points</h2>
      <p>
        This page assumes a weighted course: homework is worth 20%, the midterm
        30%, and so on, regardless of how many points each contains. That is
        the most common structure and the one syllabi usually describe.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some courses run on straight points instead — everything is worth some
        number of points and your grade is your points over the total
        available. You can still use this: enter each category's share of the
        total points as its weight. A 200-point final in a 1,000-point course
        is a 20% weight.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two systems give different answers for the same scores, which
        surprises people. Under weighted categories one bad homework mark is
        diluted by every other homework; under points it counts in proportion
        to its size. If your syllabus is ambiguous, ask which applies before
        relying on any calculation.
      </p>

      <h2>How to use it well</h2>
      <p>
        Enter every graded component, including the ones still to come, and
        leave the score blank for anything not yet graded. The tool treats
        ungraded weight as what the final has to carry, so a course with a
        20% project still outstanding and a 30% exam will tell you what those
        two together must average.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then try a few targets. The gap between what you need for an A and what
        you need for a B is often the difference between a week of work and a
        month of it, and seeing both numbers is usually more useful than
        seeing either one alone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools heading="Also worth a look" items={toolsExcept(tool.slug).map((t) => ({
      href: `/tools/${t.slug}`, title: t.title, desc: t.blurb,
    }))} />
  </ToolRoster>

  <script src="../../scripts/final-island.ts"></script>
</BaseLayout>
